      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Historians generally lean towards viewing Alcibiades as a moral failure driven by political ambition and personal vanity rather than genuine pragmatism. While his calculations of power were undeniably real, they were fueled by a calculated disregard for oath and loyalty that betrayed both his allies and friends repeatedly. Consequently, his actions were less about surviving the political system and more about seeking personal glory regardless of the moral or strategic costs.",
